   "item": {
    "subject_key": "ap-precalculus",
    "unit_number": 1,
    "topic_code": "1.12",
    "topic_title": "Transformations of Functions",
    "stem": "The point (1, 5) lies on the graph of y = f(x). Define g(x) = −2f(x−3)+4. Which point on the graph of y = g(x) corresponds to (1, 5) under these transformations?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "(−2, −6)",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Horizontal-direction reversal: this subtracts 3 from the original x-coordinate, treating f(x−3) as a shift left. Instead, the new input must satisfy x−3 = 1. Fix: Set the inside expression equal to the original input and solve for x."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "(4, −6)",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "To use the input 1 of f, solve x−3 = 1, giving x = 4. The corresponding output is −2(5)+4 = −6, so the point becomes (4, −6)."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "(4, −18)",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Outside-operation order error: this computes −2(5+4), applying the vertical shift before the multiplication. In g(x), −2 multiplies the function output before 4 is added. Fix: Multiply the original output by −2, then add 4."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "(4, 14)",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Ignored reflection: this computes 2(5)+4, dropping the negative sign on the multiplier. The factor −2 both reflects the output across the x-axis and doubles its magnitude before the shift. Fix: Include the multiplier’s sign when computing the transformed output."
     }
    ]
   },
